model {
  extend basalt.toolsGateway.poolMembership {
    metadata {
      constraint "The pool Worker's organization check refuses a pool token whose organization does not own the pool. The tombstone check stays first. Then one ownership read gives the answer: the native membership read's registration rule (the organization's pool records name the pool and the organization owns that app) and the shape read, in one read-only transaction whose statement and lock timeouts are 8 s per statement, under the pool Worker's 10 s for the whole check; a flight whose statements together run past 10 s meets the Worker's own unavailable answer. On Cloud's grouped event pool the settings, the rule and the shape read leave in one write, so the ownership read costs one round trip after the tombstone check and no separate existence read runs. The answer is 204 only when the rule finds the pool owned. It is 403 only for a completed read that finds no organization, or that names why the rule refuses: deleted-pool, cleared-association, foreign-owner, no-record or other-organization. Everything else is 503: a read that failed or that the database ended, an organization whose metadata the rule cannot read (invalid-metadata), and a refusal no shape explains. So a slow or failing database, or one organization's broken records, never refuses a pool and never lets a token through. A 403 for a shape writes one warning, Pool organization refused, with only the pool id, the organization id and the shape. invalid-metadata writes Pool organization would be refused with that shape, and a 503 the read caused writes Pool organization ownership not observed with reason timeout (any statement the database ended, the second read after a failed rule included), read-failed, changed or unexplained. Owned pools, tombstones and missing organizations write nothing, and no line carries organization metadata."
    }
  }
}
